{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1790912502,"nonce":"l8UXiT4j6uL/QGLBzw+rpo7sV+4=","message":"**Owner's ruling (2026-10-02), added to this ticket's scope — supersedes the body where they conflict.**\n\n1. **A Remedy names the write Tool that would carry it out, or says that none can.** The Investigator proposes what it judges right whether or not a configured Tool can make the change. A Remedy with no Tool shows *\"no configured Tool can carry this out\"*, **cannot be approved** (the API refuses it with a typed error, and neither the UI nor Slack offers an approve control), and can still be declined or expire. Its outbound fact says the same.\n2. **oto ships no ToolServer** (`f997185` closed). Drop any acceptance criterion that assumed a reference server checks a Remedy id back against oto. Execution is oto calling the operator's configured write Tool with the approved arguments exactly.\n3. **Docs** state that a ToolServer accepting oto's credential runs what oto sends, so oto's approval and risk rules are the only gate oto owns, and recommend narrower RBAC on a write ToolServer than on a read one.\n\n**Done when (added).** A no-Tool Remedy is created, displayed with that sentence, refused on approve (test), declinable, and expires; a Remedy whose named Tool is later removed from configuration becomes unapprovable rather than executing against nothing.","files":null}]}